@ 44.1 - Proto-Vasconic to Aquitanian
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Egurtzegi, Ander (2013), “Phonetics and Phonology”, in Basque and Proto-Basque. <https://www.academia.edu/3570162/2013a_-_Basque_and_Proto-Basque_Phonetics_and_Phonology>; Owstrowski, Manfred, “History of the Basque Language”. <http://hisp462.tamu.edu/Classes/603/Lects/BasqueHist.pdf>; Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Aquitanian langauge”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Aquitanian_language>; Wikipedia contributors (2014), ”Basque language”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Basque_language>; and Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Vasconic languages”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Vasconic_languages> ts may become ʃs or sː? The written forms are 〈xs〉 and 〈ss〉 Fortis *L is of uncertain outcome, being written as 〈l〉 or 〈ll〉 There seem to have been a few (variant?) forms which possibly show height assimilation in vowels
	ś > {{ s, s:[+long] },ʃ} / _#
	ś > s
	s > ʃ / i_#
	S:[+fortis] > S:[-voice]:[+long] (specifically, the source lists t:[+fortis] k:[+fortis]
	S:[-voice]:[+long] (specifically, the source lists t:[+fortis] k:[+fortis] > { t, t:[+long] } { k, k:[+long] }, both of the tokens with optional length suffixes and *aTa
	{ t, t:[+long] } { k, k:[+long] }, both of the tokens with optional length suffixes and *aTa > 〈atta〉, so I’m extrapolating)
	n:[+fortis] > { n, n:[+long] } / V_V
	n:[-fortis] n:[+fortis] > {n,r}(?) n
	N > :[+same POA] / _S
	r:[+fortis] > ɾ / _#
	r:[+fortis] > r
	ɡ > k / #_ (sometimes?)